14:13:44 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a genuinely reasonable thing to feel uneasy about, so let me take it seriously instead of just reassuring you it's fine. No, accepting this doesn't mean *every* gut instinct is unreliable. Your intuitions about plenty of things are probably well-calibrated — social situations you have direct experience with, judging people's character in person, physical danger, etc. Gut instincts formed from direct, repeated feedback tend to be decent. What's specifically unreliable is a narrower category: **snap judgments about groups of people you have no direct experience with, formed mostly from secondhand impressions (media silence, rankings, vague cultural osmosis) rather than actual contact or evidence.** That's a much smaller and more specific claim than "trust nothing you feel." And honestly, most people already accept this exact limitation in other domains without it feeling scary. You probably don't trust your gut about, say, whether a stock will go up, or whether a defendant is guilty just from a mugshot, or whether a medical treatment works based on how "it feels like it should." We already know intuition is a bad tool for judging things we lack direct information about, and we compensate with something else — evidence, data, deliberate checking. This is just that same category, applied to snap judgments about distant countries' populations instead of stocks or diagnoses. The scarier framing would be "I can't trust myself at all." The more accurate and much less scary framing is: "There's one identifiable blind spot — sizing up distant groups based on thin, secondhand information — and now that I can name it, I can flag it when it comes up, the same way people learn to flag their own confirmation bias or their own tendency to trust first impressions too much in job interviews." That's not a collapse of your judgment in general. It's one specific, common, well-documented glitch, now visible instead of invisible. If anything, being able to notice "oh, this is that thing" the next time you catch yourself sizing up a whole nationality off a vague impression is a pretty useful and non-scary thing to walk away with.
